{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PJqiZXzJrFYofvAxkmht5H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll use the known ICLR rating scale. Based on my knowledge and the web search results, ICLR 2026 uses a {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10} scale. The typical labels based on ICLR conventions are:\n\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n- 8: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above the acceptance threshold\n- 4: Marginally below the acceptance threshold\n- 2: Strong reject\n\n\n- 0: Trivial or wrong\n\nI'm also comparing this to the previous ICLR scales—2024/2025 used {1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10} with different mappings, so the 2026 scale represents a shift toward even-numbered ratings with adjusted thresholds.\n\nNow I'll set up the review process with two specialized reviewers handling different aspects in parallel, then move through a structured roundtable discussion starting with independent assessments, followed by cross-sharing, convergence, and finally a synthesis.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":387,"cache_read_input_tokens":59893,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":387},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f47ef9e2-3bdc-4328-afa5-da8117d03f95","uuid":"f4376400-5475-4462-af05-eaaed4f96438"}
