{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AiJEJDGsxfvVd5oc3he74r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018hwAXuZ3wSo7azF8AbK3dT","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 responds to R2's review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in an ICLR 2026 roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed a paper on applying MH-DIF to MMLU benchmarks (5,227 models × 12,508 items, finding 31.3% temporal item instability). Your preliminary score was **6 (Weak Accept)**. Your key strengths were the unprecedented scale/controls, the ground truth meta-critique, the cross-subject matching innovation, and the circularity ablation. Your key concerns were unresolved causal interpretation, high baseline temporal FPR (~31%), underpowered GSM1K analysis (N=33), and idealized semi-synthetic injection model.\n\nNow Reviewer 2 (R2) has shared their review, scoring the paper **4 (Weak Reject)**. Here are R2's key points you must respond to:\n\n**R2's Strengths (that you may not have emphasized):**\n- S1: The formulation of temporal comparability as a testable psychometric hypothesis is the real contribution, not MH-DIF itself\n- S6: Related work is comprehensive with 40+ citations\n\n**R2's Weaknesses (some overlap with yours, some new):**\n- W1 (Major): Applying unmodified MH-DIF to a new dataset sits at the boundary of \"application\" vs \"contribution.\" The 31% depends on ETS thresholds calibrated for humans.\n- W2 (Major): Causal ambiguity — similar to your W1\n- W3 (Major): GSM1K post-hoc interpretation — the direction was opposite to original hypothesis, the reinterpretation is unfalsifiable\n- W4 (Moderate): Missing engagement with prior work (tinyBenchmarks, item parameter drift literature, measurement invariance CFA)\n- W5 (Moderate): Semi-synthetic injection not sufficiently novel\n- W6 (Moderate): The redundancy finding (ρ ≈ 0.997) undercuts practical significance — if removing 31% changes nothing, why should anyone care?\n- W7 (Minor): Within-family Llama-2 vs Llama-3 C%=61.3% raises ability confounding concerns for the main temporal analysis\n\n**R2's Questions (new ones for you to consider):**\n- Q1: Why MH over logistic regression DIF as primary method?\n- Q2: If within-family (Llama-2 vs Llama-3) yields 61.3% C% due to ability confounding, why doesn't ability confounding inflate the temporal 31.3%?\n- Q6: How many independent base models are in the 5,227? If many are fine-tunes/merges, effective sample size is much smaller.\n\n**R2's Missing Related Work:**\n- Item parameter drift literature (Veerkamp & Glas 2000, Bock et al. 1988)\n- Benchmark Lottery (Dehghani 2021), Ethayarajh & Jurafsky 2020\n- Sainz et al. 2023, Jacovi et al. 2023 on indirect contamination\n- BenchMAX concurrent work\n\n**R2's Contribution/Presentation scores:** Contribution 2, Presentation 2\n\n---\n\nYour task: Write a 400-600 word response addressing R2's new points. For each point, state whether you:\n(a) agree and would lower your score\n(b) agree but do not think it changes the score\n(c) disagree and explain why\n\nState whether you want to adjust your score after seeing R2's review, and why."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4378,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8704,"cache_read_input_tokens":60280,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8704},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f47ef9e2-3bdc-4328-afa5-da8117d03f95","uuid":"9b09c53c-532e-4d44-ad62-b5a08434b9bf"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aab70a722b888d0c2","tool_use_id":"toolu_018hwAXuZ3wSo7azF8AbK3dT","description":"R1 responds to R2's review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in an ICLR 2026 roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed a paper on applying MH-DIF to MMLU benchmarks (5,227 models × 12,508 items, finding 31.3% temporal item instability). Your preliminary score was **6 (Weak Accept)**. Your key strengths were the unprecedented scale/controls, the ground truth meta-critique, the cross-subject matching innovation, and the circularity ablation. Your key concerns were unresolved causal interpretation, high baseline temporal FPR (~31%), underpowered GSM1K analysis (N=33), and idealized semi-synthetic injection model.\n\nNow Reviewer 2 (R2) has shared their review, scoring the paper **4 (Weak Reject)**. Here are R2's key points you must respond to:\n\n**R2's Strengths (that you may not have emphasized):**\n- S1: The formulation of temporal comparability as a testable psychometric hypothesis is the real contribution, not MH-DIF itself\n- S6: Related work is comprehensive with 40+ citations\n\n**R2's Weaknesses (some overlap with yours, some new):**\n- W1 (Major): Applying unmodified MH-DIF to a new dataset sits at the boundary of \"application\" vs \"contribution.\" The 31% depends on ETS thresholds calibrated for humans.\n- W2 (Major): Causal ambiguity — similar to your W1\n- W3 (Major): GSM1K post-hoc interpretation — the direction was opposite to original hypothesis, the reinterpretation is unfalsifiable\n- W4 (Moderate): Missing engagement with prior work (tinyBenchmarks, item parameter drift literature, measurement invariance CFA)\n- W5 (Moderate): Semi-synthetic injection not sufficiently novel\n- W6 (Moderate): The redundancy finding (ρ ≈ 0.997) undercuts practical significance — if removing 31% changes nothing, why should anyone care?\n- W7 (Minor): Within-family Llama-2 vs Llama-3 C%=61.3% raises ability confounding concerns for the main temporal analysis\n\n**R2's Questions (new ones for you to consider):**\n- Q1: Why MH over logistic regression DIF as primary method?\n- Q2: If within-family (Llama-2 vs Llama-3) yields 61.3% C% due to ability confounding, why doesn't ability confounding inflate the temporal 31.3%?\n- Q6: How many independent base models are in the 5,227? If many are fine-tunes/merges, effective sample size is much smaller.\n\n**R2's Missing Related Work:**\n- Item parameter drift literature (Veerkamp & Glas 2000, Bock et al. 1988)\n- Benchmark Lottery (Dehghani 2021), Ethayarajh & Jurafsky 2020\n- Sainz et al. 2023, Jacovi et al. 2023 on indirect contamination\n- BenchMAX concurrent work\n\n**R2's Contribution/Presentation scores:** Contribution 2, Presentation 2\n\n---\n\nYour task: Write a 400-600 word response addressing R2's new points. For each point, state whether you:\n(a) agree and would lower your score\n(b) agree but do not think it changes the score\n(c) disagree and explain why\n\nState whether you want to adjust your score after seeing R2's review, and why.","uuid":"2260fb1f-b41a-4511-9a29-fdb75eb31b58","session_id":"f47ef9e2-3bdc-4328-afa5-da8117d03f95"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in an ICLR 2026 roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed a paper on applying MH-DIF to MMLU benchmarks (5,227 models × 12,508 items, finding 31.3% temporal item instability). Your preliminary score was **6 (Weak Accept)**. Your key strengths were the unprecedented scale/controls, the ground truth meta-critique, the cross-subject matching innovation, and the circularity ablation. Your key concerns were unresolved causal interpretation, high baseline temporal FPR (~31%), underpowered GSM1K analysis (N=33), and idealized semi-synthetic injection model.\n\nNow Reviewer 2 (R2) has shared their review, scoring the paper **4 (Weak Reject)**. Here are R2's key points you must respond to:\n\n**R2's Strengths (that you may not have emphasized):**\n- S1: The formulation of temporal comparability as a testable psychometric hypothesis is the real contribution, not MH-DIF itself\n- S6: Related work is comprehensive with 40+ citations\n\n**R2's Weaknesses (some overlap with yours, some new):**\n- W1 (Major): Applying unmodified MH-DIF to a new dataset sits at the boundary of \"application\" vs \"contribution.\" The 31% depends on ETS thresholds calibrated for humans.\n- W2 (Major): Causal ambiguity — similar to your W1\n- W3 (Major): GSM1K post-hoc interpretation — the direction was opposite to original hypothesis, the reinterpretation is unfalsifiable\n- W4 (Moderate): Missing engagement with prior work (tinyBenchmarks, item parameter drift literature, measurement invariance CFA)\n- W5 (Moderate): Semi-synthetic injection not sufficiently novel\n- W6 (Moderate): The redundancy finding (ρ ≈ 0.997) undercuts practical significance — if removing 31% changes nothing, why should anyone care?\n- W7 (Minor): Within-family Llama-2 vs Llama-3 C%=61.3% raises ability confounding concerns for the main temporal analysis\n\n**R2's Questions (new ones for you to consider):**\n- Q1: Why MH over logistic regression DIF as primary method?\n- Q2: If within-family (Llama-2 vs Llama-3) yields 61.3% C% due to ability confounding, why doesn't ability confounding inflate the temporal 31.3%?\n- Q6: How many independent base models are in the 5,227? If many are fine-tunes/merges, effective sample size is much smaller.\n\n**R2's Missing Related Work:**\n- Item parameter drift literature (Veerkamp & Glas 2000, Bock et al. 1988)\n- Benchmark Lottery (Dehghani 2021), Ethayarajh & Jurafsky 2020\n- Sainz et al. 2023, Jacovi et al. 2023 on indirect contamination\n- BenchMAX concurrent work\n\n**R2's Contribution/Presentation scores:** Contribution 2, Presentation 2\n\n---\n\nYour task: Write a 400-600 word response addressing R2's new points. For each point, state whether you:\n(a) agree and would lower your score\n(b) agree but do not think it changes the score\n(c) disagree and explain why\n\nState whether you want to adjust your score after seeing R2's review, and why."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018hwAXuZ3wSo7azF8AbK3dT","session_id":"f47ef9e2-3bdc-4328-afa5-da8117d03f95","uuid":"90d13630-6094-41ff-9110-7ac93ac5ae12","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:16:23.227Z"}
