{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01B46YB75dJY7Wp6iD4x36UR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EA5rFg7BL9daevcX37jLH5","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks\n\n**Paper**: \"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis of MMLU\"\n\n**Venue**: ICLR 2026 | **Rating scale**: {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10}\n\n---\n\n## Panel\n\n| Reviewer | Focus | Preliminary | Post-Roundtable |\n|----------|-------|-------------|-----------------|\n| R1 | Methodology + Experiments | 6 (Weak Accept) | 6 (Weak Accept) |\n| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 4 (Weak Reject) | 5 → rounds to 6 under {0,2,4,6,8,10} |\n\n**Consolidated Overall Score: 6 — Marginally Above Acceptance Threshold (Weak Accept)**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper applies Mantel–Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis from educational psychometrics to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items). It finds that 31.3% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C, |Δ_MH| ≥ 1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. Four claims are advanced: (1) an empirical discovery of temporal item instability robust across thresholds and not driven by capability gains; (2) a meta-critique arguing web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination; (3) a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates FPR inflation; and (4) DIF as a diagnostic lens — DIF items resist contamination-based gains and are the least disruptive items for non-random removal.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Consolidated Strengths\n\n**S1. Unprecedented empirical scale with exemplary controls.** (R1 S1, R2 S2) The analysis operates on 5,227 × 12,508 — orders of magnitude larger than typical DIF studies. Controls include random-split sanity (0.18% C%), ability-quintile (2.9% C%), architecture stratification (99.98% decoder-only, Jaccard = 1.0), BH FDR correction (removes only 3/3,918 items), threshold sensitivity (10–49% across four cutoffs), and domain improvement decomposition (≤7.6% of instability explained by accuracy gains). Both reviewers agree this level of empirical diligence is exemplary.\n\n**S2. Genuine conceptual contribution in problem formulation.** (R2 S1, R1 S2) The paper's most important intellectual contribution is framing temporal score comparability as a testable psychometric hypothesis. The \"instability ≠ invalidity\" principle (ρ ≈ 0.997 after removing all DIF-C items) is nuanced and avoids over-claiming. The ground truth meta-critique — that enrichment ≈ 0.96 against web-overlap labels is *expected* rather than evidence of failure — has field-wide implications for the contamination detection literature.\n\n**S3. Cross-subject matching is a clean methodological innovation.** (R1 S3, R2 S4) The demonstration that within-subject matching inflates FPR by +0.186 while cross-subject matching achieves ΔFPR = 0.000 is convincing. This insight about matching-variable contamination is reusable beyond the specific DIF application.\n\n**S4. Circularity ablation effectively addresses tautology concerns.** (R1 S4, R2 conceded in roundtable) DIF removal is 10–16× less disruptive than accuracy-gap removal (z = −9.5 vs z = −157) despite 62.6% item overlap. This demonstrates that MH's ability conditioning genuinely filters out raw group differences, isolating differential functioning from construct-relevant difficulty.\n\n**S5. Honest treatment of limitations and negative results.** (R1 S5, R2 S5) The paper is admirably transparent about what DIF does and does not establish: the N = 33 GSM1K caveat, the RL robustness being underpowered (N = 13), the semi-synthetic simplification, and the 98% IRT misfit rate. Both reviewers credit this scientific integrity.\n\n**S6. Comprehensive experiment coverage.** All 13 planned experiments are executed and reported (4 main body, 9 appendix). The semi-synthetic dose-response (monotonic ΔTPR from p = 0.3 to p = 1.0, ΔFPR = 0.000) and the GSM8K cross-benchmark replication (ΔTPR = +0.818) provide strong validation evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consolidated Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**W1. The 31% instability finding is descriptive but causally unresolved.** (R1 W1, R2 W2 — full agreement) The paper establishes that items function differently across temporal cohorts and that domain-level accuracy improvement explains only 7.6%. But the remaining ~92% is attributed to \"item-specific changes\" without mechanistic account. The paper cannot distinguish between: (a) differential contamination, (b) changes in training methodology/data composition, (c) prompt sensitivity shifts, (d) residual ability confounding not fully removed by MH conditioning. The within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs Llama-3: 61.3% C%) raises the concern that ability confounding may inflate the temporal 31.3%, since MH conditioning on total score may be insufficient when ability gaps are large across cohorts with different distributions. The paper should discuss propensity-score matched cohorts as a direct test of this confound.\n\n**W2. The GSM1K anti-correlation (r = −0.478, N = 33) is underpowered and post-hoc.** (R1 W3, R2 W3 — full agreement) The 95% BCa CI [−0.71, −0.16] spans a wide range. Spearman CI upper bound (−0.031) barely excludes zero. The direction was opposite to the original hypothesis; the post-hoc reinterpretation (\"DIF items resist contamination-based gains\") is hard to falsify. Cook's D shows one influential model. Both reviewers agree this should be explicitly labeled as exploratory/suggestive, not confirmatory, and its weight in the narrative should be reduced. R1 notes the reinterpretation is independently testable via the Δ_MH direction distribution, partially mitigating the unfalsifiability concern.\n\n**W3. 98% IRT misfit (|Lz| > 2) is severe and insufficiently addressed.** (R1 W6, R2 reinforced in roundtable) The MH procedure conditions on total score, which implicitly assumes unidimensionality. With 98% of models violating the 2PL model, the analytic chain's statistical properties are not guaranteed. The bifactor analysis (Appendix E) reduces RMSEA from 0.035 to 0.019 and shows DIF persists at 35.8% (only 2.3pp reduction), which is reassuring but does not fully resolve whether the specific 31.3% figure is inflated or deflated by misfit. The paper's framing of this as \"typical for LLM data\" is insufficient — a sensitivity analysis quantifying the DIF-misfit interaction is needed.\n\n### Moderate\n\n**W4. Novelty sits at the boundary of \"application\" vs \"contribution.\"** (R2 W1 — R1 partially disagrees) MH-DIF is applied without modification. The three claimed contribution layers are: (a) empirical discovery, (b) ground truth critique, (c) validation framework. None involves algorithmic innovation. R1 argues the framing + infrastructure constitutes a genuine contribution; R2 contends this falls below ICLR's novelty bar. Both agree the threshold sensitivity analysis (10–49%) demonstrates that the qualitative finding is robust, but the specific 31% number depends on ETS thresholds calibrated for human populations, with no principled justification for LLMs. The panel's consensus: the paper is a strong empirical contribution but not a methodological one. Whether this suffices for ICLR is a venue-fit judgment.\n\n**W5. High baseline temporal DIF rate (~31%) limits practical utility as a contamination detector.** (R1 W2, related to R2 W6) If DIF flags 31% of items at baseline, the signal-to-noise ratio for detecting real contamination in practice is poor. The semi-synthetic framework shows DIF *can* detect injected contamination above baseline, but the practical detection regime is narrow. The paper reframes DIF as a \"diagnostic lens\" rather than a detector, which is intellectually honest, but should make this practical limitation more explicit.\n\n**W6. Semi-synthetic injection model is idealized.** (R1 W4, R2 W5 — agreement) Flipping incorrect→correct at uniform rate p is the strongest possible contamination signal. Real contamination is heterogeneous. No alternative injection models (difficulty-dependent, partial-credit, non-uniform) are tested.\n\n**W7. Missing related work on item parameter drift and measurement invariance.** (R2 W4 — R1 agrees) The paper does not engage with the item parameter drift literature (Veerkamp & Glas 2000, Bock et al. 1988), which studies exactly the temporal phenomenon measured here. Additional missing references: tinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), Benchmark Lottery (Dehghani et al. 2021), indirect contamination (Sainz et al. 2023, Jacovi et al. 2023), BenchMAX (concurrent work). The choice of MH over logistic regression DIF as the primary method is not justified.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W8. Redundancy tension.** (R2 W6 — R1 disagrees) R2 argues that ρ ≈ 0.997 undercuts practical significance: if removing 31% changes nothing, who should care? R1 counters that the diagnostic value (understanding *what changed*) does not require ranking disruption, and the redundancy itself is an informative empirical finding. Panel view: the paper handles this tension transparently via \"instability ≠ invalidity,\" but should more explicitly articulate the concrete use cases for DIF-based diagnostics.\n\n**W9. ETS thresholds not calibrated for LLMs.** (R1 W7, R2 W1 sub-point) The threshold sensitivity analysis (10–49%) mitigates this but does not resolve it. Calibrating thresholds using the random-split null distribution is a constructive suggestion.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Key Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Ability confounding quantification**: Can you run MH-DIF with propensity-score matched temporal cohorts to isolate temporal instability from cross-cohort ability distribution shift?\n\n2. **IRT misfit sensitivity**: What is the quantitative interaction between 98% |Lz| > 2 misfit and DIF flagging rates? Can you run the analysis on a dense sub-matrix or within individual subjects (using within-subject total score) to assess sensitivity?\n\n3. **Effective sample size**: How many independent base models underlie the 5,227 entries? If many are fine-tunes/merges/quantizations, what is the effective N and does MH significance hold?\n\n4. **GSM1K pre-registration**: Was the negative direction hypothesized a priori? If not, the finding should be explicitly labeled as post-hoc exploratory.\n\n5. **Why MH over logistic regression DIF?** Given the large N and potential for non-uniform DIF, logistic regression DIF seems more appropriate as the primary method. What motivated the MH choice?\n\n6. **Sparsity handling**: What is the METABENCH matrix sparsity rate and how are missing entries handled in MH computation?\n\n7. **Within-family ability confounding**: The Llama-2 vs Llama-3 comparison yields 61.3% C% attributed to ability confounding. If MH conditioning cannot fully control ability differences within families, why should it succeed for the broader temporal comparison?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll 13 planned experiments from the experiment design are covered in the paper:\n\n| Experiment | Plan ID | Location | Status |\n|------------|---------|----------|--------|\n| Real data DIF MMLU | plan_001 | §4.1, Tables 1-3 | Main body |\n| Architecture stratified DIF | plan_010 | §4.1, Table 2 | Main body |\n| Semi-synthetic validation | plan_011 | §4.3, Tables 4-5 | Main body |\n| Cross-benchmark GSM8K | plan_004 | §4.3-4.4, Tables 6-7 | Main body |\n| RL robustness | plan_002 | Appendix A, Tables 8-9 | Appendix |\n| ConStat evaluation | plan_003a | Appendix B, Table 10 | Appendix |\n| DVD evaluation | plan_003b | Appendix C, Table 11 | Appendix |\n| Ensemble fusion | plan_003c | Appendix D, Table 12 | Appendix |\n| Bifactor IRT | plan_005 | Appendix E, Tables 13-14 | Appendix |\n| Item generation | plan_006 | Appendix F, Table 15 | Appendix |\n| Cumulative Bayesian DIF | plan_007 | Appendix G, Table 16 | Appendix |\n| Graceful degradation | plan_008 | Appendix H, Table 17 | Appendix |\n| Item-Focused Trees | plan_009 | Appendix I, Tables 18-19 | Appendix |\n\nCoverage is comprehensive. The main-body/appendix allocation is appropriate: load-bearing claims (1-4) are supported by main-body experiments, supplementary evidence is in appendix.\n\n**Missing experiments identified by reviewers:**\n- Propensity-score matched temporal cohorts (addresses ability confounding)\n- Alternative semi-synthetic injection models (difficulty-dependent, partial-credit, non-uniform)\n- Dense sub-matrix analysis (sparsity sensitivity)\n- Item content analysis for DIF-C items (qualitative characterization)\n- Temporal trend with finer granularity (quarterly splits)\n\n---\n\n## 6. Consolidated Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consolidated |\n|-----------|----|----|-------------|\n| Soundness | 3 (Good) | 3 (Good) | **3 (Good)** |\n| Presentation | 3 (Good) | 2 (Fair) | **3 (Good)** — main body well-written; appendix material comprehensive |\n| Contribution | 3 (Good) | 2-3 | **3 (Good)** — strong empirical contribution at unprecedented scale; novelty is in framing + infrastructure, not algorithms |\n| Confidence | 4 (High) | 4 (High) | **4 (High)** |\n| **Overall** | **6** | **5 → 6** | **6 — Weak Accept (marginally above threshold)** |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Meta-Review Summary\n\nThis paper makes a genuine empirical contribution by bringing mature psychometric tools (MH-DIF) to an important and timely problem in LLM evaluation — temporal score comparability. The scale of the analysis (5,227 models × 12,508 items) is unprecedented, the controls are thorough, and the paper demonstrates admirable scientific integrity in its treatment of limitations and negative results. The ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap ≠ differential contamination), the cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR = 0.000), and the circularity ablation (DIF removal is 10-16× less disruptive than accuracy-gap removal) are valuable contributions that will inform future work in LLM evaluation.\n\nThe paper's primary limitations are: (1) the 31% instability finding is descriptive without causal resolution — we know items changed but not why; (2) the GSM1K external validation is underpowered (N = 33) and post-hoc; (3) the 98% IRT misfit rate raises unresolved concerns about the interaction between multidimensionality and DIF flagging; and (4) the novelty is empirical-observational rather than methodological, placing it at the boundary of ICLR's acceptance standards.\n\n**Recommendation**: Weak Accept, conditional on revisions addressing (a) propensity-score matched cohorts to quantify ability confounding, (b) IRT misfit sensitivity analysis, (c) explicit GSM1K framing as exploratory, and (d) engagement with the item parameter drift literature. The paper's core finding — that ~30% of MMLU items function differently across model generations, independent of threshold choice — is robust and informative for the LLM evaluation community.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Actionable Revision Priorities\n\n1. **[Critical]** Add propensity-score matched temporal cohorts analysis to quantify residual ability confounding in the 31% figure\n2. **[Critical]** Provide sensitivity analysis of DIF flagging rates under varying IRT misfit levels\n3. **[High]** Reframe GSM1K correlation as explicitly exploratory/suggestive; reduce its narrative weight\n4. **[High]** Add item parameter drift literature (Veerkamp & Glas 2000, Bock et al. 1988) and justify MH over logistic regression DIF\n5. **[Medium]** Report effective sample size (independent base models) and METABENCH sparsity handling\n6. **[Medium]** Discuss ETS threshold calibration for LLM populations; consider random-split null calibration\n7. **[Low]** Test alternative injection models (difficulty-dependent, non-uniform) in semi-synthetic framework\n8. **[Low]** Add qualitative item content analysis for a sample of DIF-C items","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260521T042647","overall_score":"6 - Weak Accept","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1839,"cache_read_input_tokens":78016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1839},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f47ef9e2-3bdc-4328-afa5-da8117d03f95","uuid":"30fd2374-95ea-4a6f-8cc1-56499eab875e"}
